  [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/[39m, and get your sign-off before any code.

  One scope flag up front: notifications presuppose three things this repo doesn't have — tasks with state, a notion of
  distinct users, and a source of change events. If none of those exist yet, the honest decomposition is [3mtasks first, [0m
  [3mnotifications second[0m, and what we design now is the notification piece plus the minimum contract it needs from the
  task layer.

  [1mFirst fork — where does "change" happen?[0m This decides nearly everything downstream and is expensive to reverse:

  - [1mSingle-user, browser-only.[0m Changes originate from one person in one tab. "Notifications" means in-app feedback about
    your own edits (undo toasts, "3 tasks due today"). No server, no auth, no delivery. Cheap, but rules out ever
    notifying someone about [3msomeone else's[0m change without a rewrite.
  - [1mMulti-user with a backend.[0m Changes originate from other people, so notifications need identity, subscriptions,
    persistence, and a delivery channel. Much bigger; it's the only version where "users get notified when tasks they
    care about change" means what it usually means.
  - [1mSingle-user, multi-device.[0m One person, changes sync across their own devices. Needs a server and sync but no sharing
    or permissions model. Middle ground.

  My recommendation: tell me it's the second one only if real multi-user sharing is genuinely the goal — otherwise the
  first is a fraction of the work and the honest fit for a one-page app.
